---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { withBase } from '../lib/content';
---
<Base title="Editorial standards · The Daily Brief" description="The public sourcing and writing standards for The Daily Brief.">
  <main id="main">
    <section class="masthead" aria-labelledby="standards-title">
      <div class="masthead-row">
        <h1 id="standards-title">The desk</h1>
        <p class="tagline">A public standard for compressed, source-led writing.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="edition-strip"><span>Editorial standards</span><span class="muted"><a href={withBase('/')}>Latest issue</a></span></div>
    </section>
    <section class="standards">
      <div class="eyebrow">Publication note</div>
      <h2>What gets printed</h2>
      <div class="standards-grid">
        <div>
          <h3>Source before flourish</h3>
          <p>Every dispatch names its source, date, and direct reading link. Summaries stay within the evidence available from the source. When a claim is moving, disputed, or thin, the language says so.</p>
          <h3>Compressed, not flattened</h3>
          <p>The desk reduces a piece to its useful shape without pretending that a paper, a discussion thread, a podcast, and a personal essay are the same kind of object.</p>
          <h3>Public by construction</h3>
          <p>The public edition contains no private-feed labels, saved-note metadata, personal reactions, account identity, or reader profiling. Selection can happen privately; publication remains neutral.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Copy rules</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Concrete headlines; no clickbait or inflated certainty.</li>
            <li>No generic filler such as “this article explores” or “game-changing.”</li>
            <li>No advice addressed to a reader and no simulated personal voice.</li>
            <li>Short quotations remain exact and traceable.</li>
            <li>Editorial synthesis is kept distinct from source fact.</li>
            <li>Every item needs a deep link, not only a headline.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3>Evidence marks</h3>
          <p><code>checked</code> means the desk has a primary source or paper. <code>reported</code> means the item is a public report or feed signal. <code>contested</code>, <code>moving</code>, and <code>thin</code> preserve uncertainty rather than hiding it.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
